You have set up the following equality using the property of exponents:
\begin{equation*}
(2^3)^{(2^3)} = 2^N
\end{equation*}
To solve for $N$, we need to simplify the left side of the equation. We can do this by applying another rule of exponents, which states that:
$\begin{aligned} (x^m)^n =& x^{mn} \end{aligned}$

Applying this rule to our equation, we get:
\begin{align*}
(2^3)^{(2^3)} =& (2^3)^8\\
\end{align*}

Now, we can simplify the right side of the equation by multiplying the exponents:
\begin{align*}
(2^3)^8 =& 2^{3\cdot8}\\
=& 2^{24}
\end{align*}

Therefore, we have:
\begin{equation*} \boxed{2^{24}} = 2^N \end{equation*}

So, $N=\boxed{24}$.

