The roots of $x^3 + Px^2 + Qx - 19 = 0$ are $a+1, b+1, c+1,$ while the roots of $x^3 - Ax^2 + Bx - C = 0$ are $a, b, c.$  By Vieta's formulas, we have:
\begin{align*}
-(a + 1) - (b + 1) - (c + 1) &= a + b + c, \\
(a + 1)(b + 1) + (b + 1)(c + 1) + (c + 1)(a + 1) &= ab + bc + ca, \\
-(a)(b)(c) &= -19.
\end{align*}Expanding and simplifying, we get:
\begin{align*}
-a - b - c - 3 &= a + b + c, \\
ab + ac + bc + 2(a + b + c) + 3 &= ab + bc + ca, \\
abc &= 19.
\end{align*}From the first equation, we have $-2a - 2b - 2c = 3,$ or $a + b + c = -\frac{3}{2}.$  Thus, $A = a + b + c = \boxed{-\frac{3}{2}}.$

Evaluate {eq}\, \displaystyle  \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{(2 + cos x)(2 + cos (2x))}.\,

{/eq}
{eq}\begin{align*}

\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{(2 + cos x)(2 + cos (2x))}

& =  \lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \frac{1}{(2 + cos (x))(2 + cos (2\cdot x))}

\\

& =\frac{1}{(2 + cos (0))(2 + cos (2\cdot 0))} & \left ( \cos (0) =1\right )

\\

& =\frac{1}{(2 + 1)(2 + 1)}

\\

& =\frac{1}{3\cdot 3}

\\

& =\frac{1}{9}

\end{align*}

{/eq}


Therefore, the answer is   {eq}f(x) =\lim_{x\rightarrow 0} \displaystyle  \frac{1}{(2 + cos x)(2 + cos (2x))}= \displaystyle \frac{1}{9}.

{/eq}

